# PostgreSQL + Prisma Best Practices

When working with PostgreSQL and Prisma, design for correctness, performance, and high concurrency.

## Schema Design

- Think in PostgreSQL first, Prisma second.
- Use correct types:
  - Money → Decimal (Prisma) mapped to NUMERIC (Postgres)
  - Never use Float/REAL for financial values
- Always define:
  - Primary keys (@id)
  - Unique constraints (@unique)
  - Defaults (@default)
  - Timestamps (@updatedAt, now())
- Enforce constraints at database level (NOT NULL, CHECK, FK)
- Use explicit relation fields
- Add indexes for frequently queried fields

Example model:

model Bet {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  userId    String
  stake     Decimal  @db.Numeric(12,2)
  odds      Decimal  @db.Numeric(8,3)
  status    BetStatus
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  @@index([userId])
  @@index([status])
}

## Query Practices

- Avoid N+1 queries → use include/select
- Fetch only required fields
- Prefer set-based operations over loops
- Always paginate large datasets

Good:

await prisma.bet.findMany({
  where: { status: "OPEN" },
  select: { id: true, stake: true, odds: true }
})

## Transactions & Integrity

- Always use transactions for:
  - balance updates
  - bet placement
  - payouts
- Ensure atomic operations

Example:

await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
  const user = await tx.user.findUnique({
    where: { id: userId }
  })

  if (!user || user.balance.lt(stake)) {
    throw new Error("Insufficient funds")
  }

  await tx.user.update({
    where: { id: userId },
    data: { balance: { decrement: stake } }
  })

  await tx.bet.create({
    data: { userId, stake, odds }
  })
})

## Concurrency (Critical)

- Always assume high concurrency
- Prevent race conditions:
  - Use SELECT FOR UPDATE when needed
  - Do not rely only on Prisma abstractions

Example:

await prisma.$queryRaw`
  SELECT * FROM "User"
  WHERE id = ${userId}
  FOR UPDATE
`

- Keep transactions short

## Performance

- Add indexes in Prisma:

@@index([userId, status])

- Analyze queries with EXPLAIN ANALYZE
- Avoid:
  - SELECT *
  - repeated queries in loops
  - unnecessary subqueries

- Use raw SQL for:
  - aggregations
  - window functions
  - complex joins

## PostgreSQL Features

Use when appropriate:

- CTEs (WITH)
- Window functions
- JSONB (only when needed)
- UPSERT

Example UPSERT:

INSERT INTO users (id, email)
VALUES (1, 'test@test.com')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE
SET email = EXCLUDED.email;

Partial index:

CREATE INDEX idx_active_users
ON users (id)
WHERE status = 'active';

## Security

- Always use parameterized queries
- Never concatenate SQL strings

Bad:

await prisma.$queryRawUnsafe("SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '" + email + "'")

Good:

await prisma.$queryRaw`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ${email}`

## Critical Rules

- Never use floating point for money
- Never update balances without a transaction
- Never rely only on app logic → enforce in DB
- Always design for auditability
- Always assume concurrent writes

## Migrations

- Review migrations before applying
- Add indexes manually if missing
- Avoid destructive changes in production
- Backup before major changes

## Production

- Use connection pooling (PgBouncer)
- Monitor slow queries
- Tune:
  - shared_buffers
  - work_mem
- Run VACUUM and ANALYZE regularly

## Advanced Tip

- Prefer ledger-based systems for financial logic
- Store immutable transaction records
- Derive balances instead of mutating blindly

